WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.660
So today I will be
talking about

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:14.190
clustering in tidymodels.
It's something

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:17.130
I've been working with
Kelly Bodwin with

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.710
for the last half year or so.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:23.340
And I'm very excited to come
here and talk to all of you

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.640
about it.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:28.280
So before we go any
further, I want

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:30.830
to give a quick
additional introduction

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:35.270
to what I find most
important about tidymodels

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:36.830
as a framework.

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:39.620
And the three most
important things I see

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:44.030
is that it's consistent,
modular, and extensible.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:46.490
It's consistent in a way
that any function that

00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:49.160
returns a tibble will
always return a tibble

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:51.510
if it returns a fitted object

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:52.730
it will always be a fitted object

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:56.490
And any things like dimensions.

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:58.180
with respect to column

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:02.220
Names and raw numbers
will always stay the same.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.010
So once you know what
a function outputs,

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:10.100
you can reliably code knowing
that will be the case.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.460
Certainly it's
modular in the sense

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:17.630
that instead of having
one monster function

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:20.780
that will do a bunch
of preprocessing bit of

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.180
modeling inside a
cross-validation fold

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:26.390
and find the most optimal model.

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:31.190
According to some heuristics, we
instead create smaller objects

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:34.750
that you can then bind
together

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:38.450
Allowing us to easily.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:43.460
Swap out and build a
more complete workflow.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:47.540
And lastly, and for
me, most importantly,

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:51.180
is the idea of extensibility.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:55.440
So tidymodels was built
from the ground up knowing

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:59.910
that we needed to make
it so other people could

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:04.710
build extensions and to make.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:08.190
it much more
maintainable instead

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:12.240
of having one large part of
hundreds of thousands of code

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.460
that needs to all
be kept in sync.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:18.510
Instead, we have smaller,
more focused packages

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:21.030
that could be
handled independently,

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:25.450
yet they all still work
wonderfully together.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:27.430
This part is near
and dear to my heart

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:29.740
because this is how
I started working

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:32.000
with tidymodels
four years ago

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.070
When I started working on
my first extension pattern.

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:39.190
And now it let me up here
where I will talk about

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:43.890
another extension package
in front of all of you.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.740
So when we think about
machine learning,

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:51.510
it's a wonderfully
ill-defined topic.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:55.080
But one of the ways
we can think about it

00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:00.070
is in the idea of unsupervised
and unsupervised setting.

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:05.420
So tidymodels right
now worked really well

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:06.990
in the supervised setting.

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:10.130
We can do classification,
we can do regression

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:12.150
as we just saw with.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.300
We also are starting
to work on tools

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:17.150
to deal with censored
regression, which isn't even

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.320
on this diagram.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:22.760
We also have tools in
the form of preprocessing

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:25.220
to deal with
dimensionality reduction,

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:27.050
having a lot of
variables turn it

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:30.570
into a smaller, hopefully
better set of variables.

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:32.220
But there's something
very glaring

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:34.260
right in the middle
of my diagram

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.690
right here that we haven't
been able to do for the longest

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:38.010
time.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:40.200
And that's

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:43.470
what drove me to
work on this project

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:46.650
is we want to work
with clustering,

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:48.490
but it just didn't fit in.

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:51.880
And it wasn't even little
hacky to make it work.

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:59.310
It wasn't possible before to do
any kind of clustering models.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:02.520
That's why I'm very
excited to gave

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.820
the first public
announcement that we're

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:07.695
introducing the tidyclust package.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:21.725
So you're probably going to
have a couple of questions

00:04:21.725 --> 00:04:23.350
or you're thinking,
something you like.

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:25.600
Yes finally, I needed this.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:27.650
For years, people
have been asking.

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:29.350
People are trying to teach it.

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:33.540
We have a lot of people that
try to teach the classic intro

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:35.490
to statistics or
machine learning,

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:38.770
and you can do
almost everything.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:41.360
in tidymodels and
then you have one.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:42.980
week where you show clustering.

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:45.770
And it just feels all.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:46.950
We're finally here.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:49.550
Some of you might think
I really appreciate

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:53.850
that he's dressing in the
same theme as the slides.

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:56.600
But lastly.

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:59.710
A maybe more important
question is why

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:02.710
we're building another package.

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:05.830
And that comes from
some internal friction,

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:08.590
as I mentioned before,
that tidymodels just

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:11.020
wasn't built to do clustering.

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:14.110
It was built with
supervised models in mind.

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:18.410
And there wasn't really any
way to force it through.

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:20.990
It just didn't work.

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:24.980
And this is because of some
fundamental differences

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:28.070
between how
clustering models work

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:33.990
and how a typical
supervised models work.

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:38.600
And the main thing, which in
essence is a very fundamental

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:42.160
property, and it seems a
little bit simple

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:45.880
Most models have
outcomes and yeah,

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:47.150
of course they have outcomes.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:49.540
But it meant that
every single piece

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:56.410
of code we have, more or less, is
written with outcomes in mind.

00:05:56.410 --> 00:05:58.810
And it just meant that it
didn't work like we couldn't

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:00.200
make it work.

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:03.910
Because not only do you
clustering models not

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:07.680
have outcomes, we don't want
you to put in outcomes because

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:13.270
It would be a symptom you are
trying to do the wrong thing.

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:16.270
We also have in tidymodels
the idea

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:18.760
that we have clearly
defined predictions

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:21.770
which are in clustering a
very controversial topic

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:23.590
It will still talk
about it, but it still

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:27.940
is not the clearly most
clearly defined thing.

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:31.600
And lastly, related to
the idea of outcomes,

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:35.050
having a ground truth to
test your model against

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:40.200
means that you didn't
do that performance checking.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:44.500
You can have a model, you
hide the outcome, predict on it

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:50.380
and see; do my predictions
align with the true values?

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:53.920
That made it very easy to do
normal performance metrics.

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:58.170
We can do no such thing
with clustering methods.

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:01.680
There are some ways
of seeing can we make.

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:05.020
But does this clustering
model appear to be solid?

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:08.370
But we don't have
any clear answer.

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:14.440
Nevertheless, we still move on
and try to do the best we can.

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:15.585
This means that.

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:21.970
We needed to do a
lot of work to port

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:25.240
over many of the
internals of tidymodels,

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:31.210
rewrite them from scratch
with the assumption

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:33.190
that there's no outcomes.

00:07:33.190 --> 00:07:36.940
So it's a lot of parallel
code, but hopefully it

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:42.370
means for all of you that it
will be a seamless transition

00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:44.290
as possible.

00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:47.890
If you're already familiar with
tidymodels, i would wager that

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:51.470
by reading the vignettes we
have coming up later today.

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:54.610
On the pkgdown site,
you can get up

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:59.770
and running with tidyclust
in an hour or two.

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:01.760
because

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:05.190
We found a fairly
good transition.

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:07.860
To show how some of
this work I will use

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:10.870
some very exciting data set.

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:14.880
So it's a data set of
dates as in the fruit.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:17.310
And it is

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:19.570
Features extracted from images.

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:23.130
So they have a bunch of
images of dates and then they

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:27.500
calculated various things of
those

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:31.670
Images so that the area
will be the number of pixels

00:08:31.670 --> 00:08:33.210
that contain the fruit.

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:35.970
We have perimeter, eccentricity.

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:44.300
We also have various
things about the RGB

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:45.840
of those images.

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:48.750
So there's a lot
happening in here.

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:52.550
And we're hoping to
see if just by having

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:56.320
these visual metrics about.

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:00.680
These dates if we can
pull out some clusters.

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:03.410
Two things you will
notice about this data is

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:07.610
some of these values have
quite different ranges.

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:09.530
So something like the
area is in the order

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:13.490
of hundreds of thousands and
something like eccentricity

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:15.030
is quite a low value.

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:22.010
So if our clustering model has
any type of scale sensitivity,

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:23.570
we need to deal with that.

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:26.810
Additionally, we
have a lot of these.

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:29.480
Predictors being
quite correlated.

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:32.040
So generally for most shapes.

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:35.030
We have that the area
parameter convex

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:38.270
areas of roughly related
and likewise something

00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:41.660
like to tell us if there's a lot
of blue in a picture that might

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:43.130
not be as much of the other color.

00:09:43.130 --> 00:09:46.490
So we have a lot of
correlations which we can

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:48.285
see in this correlation matrix.

00:09:48.285 --> 00:09:49.910
It's a little too
small to actually see

00:09:49.910 --> 00:09:51.440
what different
things are, but we

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:56.810
are seeing huge chunks of highly
correlated variables and highly

00:09:56.810 --> 00:09:58.850
decorrelated data
variables, so we might

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:01.860
want to have to deal
with that as well.

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:05.130
So we start out the same
way we do in parsnip

00:10:05.130 --> 00:10:08.610
by creating a clustering
model specification.

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:12.730
So here we are doing a simple
k-means and we write it out.

00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:15.960
And with that k-means
Cluster specification.

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:18.130
We have yet another mode here.

00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:20.700
We talk about a partition.

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:25.860
So we say a partition is
when every observation will

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:29.330
be assigned one and
only one cluster.

00:10:29.330 --> 00:10:30.300
Later down the line.

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:34.790
We want to expand
our work surface

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:39.130
to use extractive
clustering where.

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:44.050
Any one observation can
be in 0 or more clusters.

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:47.620
So there is work on that
in the horizon.

00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:54.010
But how we can specify a
cluster specification here using

00:10:54.010 --> 00:10:56.530
the default engine
in the stats package.

00:10:56.530 --> 00:10:59.450
We can also set the
number of clusters.

00:10:59.450 --> 00:11:03.070
We can change the engine or
add engine specific things.

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:05.650
Another thing we
really try to work

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:12.480
hard on as we do in parsnip
and other packages is have.

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:14.280
More descriptive names.

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:18.540
So instead of having k
and h, we will instead

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:22.110
name it something like
num_clusters or tot_height.

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:27.930
So you don't have to as
much jargon moving around.

00:11:27.930 --> 00:11:31.320
With this model
specification, we

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:34.710
can now work with the
rest of tidymodels.

00:11:34.710 --> 00:11:37.230
So here we are creating
a specification

00:11:37.230 --> 00:11:40.010
and we said we wanted
to normalize the data.

00:11:40.010 --> 00:11:43.980
I'm dealing with
the correlations

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:53.310
by applying some PCA keeping
the most variable principal

00:11:53.310 --> 00:11:56.290
components and we
can do it easy.

00:11:56.290 --> 00:11:59.700
Put it into a workflow
and fit this workflow.

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:02.190
And if you take a
quick note, you will

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:05.610
see that this is very,
very similar to everything

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:07.300
else we're doing in tidymodels.

00:12:07.300 --> 00:12:11.070
The only two
differences is instead

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:17.250
of specifying as a parsnip
object we instead creating

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:19.860
our tidyclust object.

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:23.580
And in the recipe we are
not specifying an outcome.

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:26.970
But everything else
should work smoothly.

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:31.840
So there's very little new
things for you to learn.

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:35.780
Once we have a fitted workflow,
we now have an object.

00:12:35.780 --> 00:12:38.740
We want to do certain
calculations with it

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:41.700
straight out, see
what happens inside.

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:43.920
Traditionally a lot
of that work was

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:50.370
delegated into broom package,
while amazing, has been drawing

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:52.870
a lot and have drawing pain.

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:56.460
So we are rewriting a
lot of that functionality

00:12:56.460 --> 00:13:02.520
on our new engines to
stay consistent and work

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:04.500
the way we want it to.

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:06.420
So some of the
things we can do

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:12.120
is pull out what cluster
each of the observations

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:15.180
were found in our
cluster model.

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:19.770
We always did a tibble
back here named .cluster,

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:21.790
so it's always the same.

00:13:21.790 --> 00:13:29.410
Additionally, we have done
some reconciliation

00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:31.160
of the cluster names.

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:34.390
So in a case like k-means
the cluster is a random,

00:13:34.390 --> 00:13:37.840
but we are stopping some of that
randomness from coming through.

00:13:37.840 --> 00:13:41.640
So you're more likely to.

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:47.170
actually have consistent
clusters across bootstraps.

00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:48.820
Another thing we
can do is pull out

00:13:48.820 --> 00:13:50.620
where those classes are located.

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:54.340
We can do that with
extract_centroids.

00:13:54.340 --> 00:13:56.860
But then we can note
here that since we

00:13:56.860 --> 00:14:00.640
had a workflow with a recipe,

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:06.730
the centroid are based
on where the data.

00:14:06.730 --> 00:14:09.400
Centroids are based in
the data space that

00:14:09.400 --> 00:14:12.340
was passed to the cluster
model, which in this case

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:15.180
was the PCs that came
out of the recipe.

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:21.460
And lastly, we are
a little on the edge

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:24.340
by saying that some
of these engines

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:26.330
also allow for prediction.

00:14:26.330 --> 00:14:29.860
So prediction is a little
bit of a touchy subject,

00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:34.840
but in every type of
models, we have

00:14:34.840 --> 00:14:37.190
to describe what we
actually mean by it.

00:14:37.190 --> 00:14:43.810
And in the case of k-means
it means which cluster

00:14:43.810 --> 00:14:47.170
centroid are you closest to?

00:14:47.170 --> 00:14:50.340
And this works.

00:14:50.340 --> 00:14:52.720
again with the
whole workflow.

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:54.850
So the preprocessing
will happen.

00:14:54.850 --> 00:14:58.290
So you don't have so you
don't have those sneaky data

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:00.990
leakage that happens
by doing PC before

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:03.930
and everything like that.

00:15:03.930 --> 00:15:09.180
While I talked about how
something like metrics

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:11.400
can be quite hard to
calculate, there still

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:15.220
is a number of metrics
that we would like to do.

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:19.810
So in the same
style as yardstick,

00:15:19.810 --> 00:15:22.600
we have a number
of metrics that are

00:15:22.600 --> 00:15:25.890
specific to these
cluster fits.

00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:31.860
So here we have a function that
calculates the total of total

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:34.960
in sum of squared errors.

00:15:34.960 --> 00:15:39.990
And to calculate that
it's not just a matter

00:15:39.990 --> 00:15:44.860
of comparing an outcome to.

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:46.450
A ground truth.

00:15:46.450 --> 00:15:49.690
Instead, we are
calculating a metric

00:15:49.690 --> 00:15:53.860
based on the centroid
and the observations

00:15:53.860 --> 00:15:57.550
that this metric is summing up.

00:15:57.550 --> 00:16:02.410
How far the points
are from the clusters.

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:05.470
And in the same style,
if we have multiple,

